🔍 Analysis of Your Current Bases

1. What You Have Covered (The Forward Pass)
By plugging the real-estate agents into your automation simulation, you have successfully captured the Transactional Node.
Real-estate agents hold the data for velocity (how fast units sell), yield (market value), and demographics (who is moving in).
They are the outward-facing edge of the property lifecycle.


2. The Logic Gap (The Backpropagation Problem)
In machine learning and network simulation, backpropagation passes the error gradient (or downstream impact) backward from the output layer to the source weights.
The Issue: Real-estate agents do not inherit or generate the structural carbon footprint of a building; they merely trade the finished product.
If you backpropagate "carbon deletions" (which is an excellent phrase—implying stripping out embodied carbon or subtracting environmental debt) directly from market transactions, you miss the actual physical conversion layer.



🛠️ How to Fully Cover Your Bases
To make your automation simulation bulletproof, you need to plug three missing operational layers into your network graph before backpropagating back to the developers:
The Embodied Carbon Node (The Supply Chain): Developers like Wolfbrook, Titus, or Skyline don't physically manufacture concrete or cross-laminated timber. To accurately simulate a "carbon deletion," your algorithm needs to backpropagate past the developer and hit the materials suppliers (e.g., Firth Concrete, Fletcher Building).
The Operational Carbon Node (The Council/Zoning): The long-term carbon footprint of Glendowie’s medium-density housing is heavily dictated by Auckland Council's unitary plan (infrastructure, stormwater, public transport access). The simulation should weigh whether the development reduces per-capita transport emissions.
The Temporal Disconnect: Real-estate agents operate in real-time (days/weeks on the market). Developers operate in project-time (2–3 year build cycles). Carbon deletions operate in climate-time (50–100 year lifecycles). Your automation simulation must normalize these three vastly different time-series scales, or your backpropagation gradients will explode or vanish.



